MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level1_TheFirstCase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_1
  levelNumber: 1
  displayName: The First Case
  sceneName: Game
  description: "Your first day on the case. Texts and emails arrive while you explore
    the house \u2014 decide which are scams."
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3c2461ec3d7cb3f4195d1800bbe3a169, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: CASE BRIEFING
  briefingText: 'Welcome to your first case. This room has six questions: four case
    questions with four answer choices and two messages or web pages to judge as
    SCAM or LEGITIMATE. They arrive in a different order on every attempt, and the
    answer choices are shuffled too, so read each one carefully instead of remembering
    positions.'
  caseNotes:
  - You have not ordered anything this month.
  objectives:
  - Answer all six questions in this room.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose.
  - Score 380 or more to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: E01
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: Ana typed her student login into a fake "school portal" page. The scammer
      can now read her grades, messages and personal details. <b>Which part of the
      CIA Triad was broken?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Confidentiality means only authorized people can see information.
      The scammer was never authorized, so confidentiality is broken. Integrity would
      be broken if the scammer <i>changed</i> her data, and availability if she were
      <i>locked out</i>. Being tricked into giving the password away does not make
      the access authorized.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Availability
    - Integrity
    - Confidentiality
    - None, because she typed it in herself
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: 'Correct. A stolen password breaks <b>confidentiality</b>: someone
      who shouldn''t see your data now can.'
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 3:
      Fundamentals of Information Security (CIA Triad)"
  - id: E02
    channel: 0
    sender: +63 917 555 0142
    senderDetail: 
    subject: 
    body: "SwiftParcel: Your package is ON HOLD due to an incomplete address. Pay
      the \u20B129 redelivery fee within 12 hours or it will be returned:"
    link: http://swiftparcel-redeliver.example/pay
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: You have not ordered anything this month.
    explanation: 'You are not expecting a package. It comes from a regular mobile
      number, sets a 12-hour deadline, and links to a domain that isn''t the courier''s.
      The small fee is bait: the real goal is your card or e-wallet details. Couriers
      don''t collect fees through random text links.'
    trigger: 3
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: "Scam. No order, a small 'fee' and a 12-hour deadline. They want your
      payment details, not \u20B129."
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 16:
      Information Security Best Practices (user awareness)"
  - id: E03
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: '<b>Email from:</b> Campus IT Helpdesk <noparse><</noparse>helpdesk@uphs1-binan.example<noparse>></noparse>

      <b>Subject:</b>
      Mailbox 98% full: verify now

      "Your school mailbox will be DELETED in
      24 hours. Click below and sign in to verify your account." <b>What kind of
      threat is this?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'It pretends to be a trusted sender, creates fear with "deleted
      in 24 hours", and asks you to sign in through a link. That combination is phishing.
      The domain is a lookalike: <b>uphs1</b> with the number 1 in place of the letter
      <b>l</b>. Real storage warnings don''t need you to "verify" your password through
      an email link.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Phishing
    - Ransomware
    - Denial-of-service attack
    - A normal storage warning
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 'Phishing. Look closely: <b>uphs1</b>, not uphsl. A fake sender plus
      a fake deadline plus ''sign in here'' means phishing.'
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Introduction to Computer Security:
      \"types of security threats\u2026 in both personal and organizational contexts\""
  - id: E04
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: 'A caller says: "This is ExampleBank''s fraud department. Someone is using
      your card right now. We just sent you a 6-digit code. Read it to me so we can
      block the transaction."'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: A one-time code (OTP) proves that <i>you</i> are approving something.
      Whoever has it can approve a login or payment as you, so the caller is probably
      the one making the transaction. A real bank never asks you to read an OTP aloud.
      Calling back on a number you already trust is the safe way to check.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Read the code quickly to stop the fraud
    - Give only the first three digits
    - Ask them to email you instead
    - Hang up and call the number on the back of your card or in the official app
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Hang up. <b>No real bank asks for your OTP.</b> Call back using the
      number on your card.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Authentication and Access
      Control"
  - id: E05
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: 'A stranger in a gaming chat sends you <b>FreeSkins_Generator.exe</b> and
      says: "Turn off your antivirus first. It gives a false alarm on this tool."'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'Asking you to turn off your antivirus is a major warning sign:
      the file is built to get past it. Fake "generators" are a common way to spread
      trojans and information stealers, which take saved passwords and logged-in
      sessions. Scanning afterwards is too late, and sending it to a friend puts
      them at risk.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Turn off the antivirus just for a minute and run it
    - Run it, then scan your PC afterwards
    - Don't download it, and block or report the sender
    - Send it to a friend to test first
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: Don't run it. Anyone who asks you to switch off your antivirus is trying
      to get past it.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Malware and Threats: \"preventing,
      detecting, and removing malware, including\u2026 anti-malware tools\""
  - id: E06
    channel: 2
    sender: Viral post
    senderDetail: 
    subject: FIND YOUR STAR NAME
    body: Comment your first pet's name + the street you grew up on + your birth
      month!
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: Is this harmless fun or a scam? LEGITIMATE = harmless fun, SCAM
      = data harvesting.
    explanation: Pet names, childhood streets and birth months are common answers
      to account security questions. Posting them publicly gives scammers what they
      need to reset your passwords or make an impersonation more convincing. The
      "game" is how they collect the answers.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Data harvesting. Those are classic <b>security-question answers</b>.
      Don't post them.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Weeks 10\u201311:
      Ethics for IT Users (\"responsible use of\u2026 social media\")"
